<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 850–1204 (the middle Byzantine period, from the Macedonian emperors to the Fourth Crusade)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Emperor to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="Hagia Sophia"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a><span class="soc-credit">Adli Wahid · CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> called themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> (Rhomaioi), and their emperor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>, was held to be God’s chosen ruler and the living law. Rank flowed above all from him: the court awarded titles and offices in a strict order of precedence, set out in lists such as the Kletorologion of Philotheos (899), with separate ranks for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>. Beside this hierarchy stood the Church’s, from the patriarch down to the village priest and the monks. Most people were peasants, many of them free owners in village communities that answered for the land tax together, others tenants of great landlords, and some households owed military service for their land. In the 10th century emperors legislated to protect small farmers from “the powerful” (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynatoi">dynatoi</a>), but in the 11th and 12th centuries a few aristocratic families, closely tied to the ruling Komnenoi, came to dominate the state and its land.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 850–1204 (the middle Byzantine period, from the Macedonian emperors to the Fourth Crusade)</span><span><b>People</b> 12–18 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 10</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Basileus</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Dynatoi</span> <span class="soc-en">(The powerful)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.05–0.2% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Episkopoi</span> <span class="soc-en">(The patriarch and bishops)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Logothetai</span> <span class="soc-en">(Officials)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.1–0.3% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Stratiotai</span> <span class="soc-en">(Soldiers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">3–6%</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Monachoi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Monks, nuns and parish clergy)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:3%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Emporoi kai technitai</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants and craftsmen of the guilds)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–4% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:66%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Georgoi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free peasants)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 66% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-dependent soc-est" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:22%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Paroikoi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Dependent peasants)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 15–30% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-10" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Douloi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
